Postural Orthostatic Tachycardia Syndrome (POTS)
Cardiovascular
Postural lightheadedness/presyncope, Extreme tachycardia on standing, Dependent acrocyanosis, Heart rate increase >30 bpm on tilt-table

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CDV-034
Reason for Visit: Debilitating dizziness, rapid heart rate, and near-fainting every time the patient stands up, severely impacting daily life.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and parent
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a young patient completely immobilized by severe postural tachycardia and presyncope. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to clinically differentiate this severe dysautonomia from an acute tachyarrhythmia or significant hypovolemia, perform active stand testing to confirm hemodynamic parameters, and provide immediate management strategies to restore basic mobility prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "Whenever I stand up, my heart races to 140 beats a minute, my head spins, and my legs turn a purplish color."
History of Present Illness: Patient (aged 22) reports a 6-month history of profound lightheadedness and presyncope strictly provoked by assuming an upright posture. Over the last week, symptoms have become debilitating. Reports extreme symptomatic tachycardia upon standing, accompanied by tremulousness and feeling jittery. Notes chronic generalized fatigue, severe exercise intolerance, and "brain fog." Reports dependent acrocyanosis (reddish-purple discoloration) of the lower extremities when standing for more than a few minutes. Immediate relief of symptoms upon resuming a supine position. KEY SYMPTOMS: Profound lightheadedness and presyncope strictly provoked by assuming an upright posture. Extreme symptomatic tachycardia upon standing without a concurrent drop in blood pressure. Dependent acrocyanosis. Immediate relief of symptoms upon resuming a supine position.
Functional Status: Currently bedbound; unable to shower or stand to prepare food without severe symptoms.
Social/Safety Concerns: High anxiety and severe disruption to university studies and daily functioning.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP Supine: 110/70 | HR Supine: 75 | BP Standing (10 mins): 115/75 | HR Standing (10 mins): 125 | Temp: 36.6 C | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99 percent | Wt: 60 Kg
General: Appears comfortable and calm when supine; becomes visibly distressed, pale, and tremulous upon standing.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Regular rhythm. Structurally normal heart, no murmurs. Active stand testing reveals a sustained increase in heart rate of 50 beats per minute within 10 minutes of standing, without a concurrent drop in blood pressure. Extremities: Clear reddish-purple discoloration (dependent acrocyanosis) develops in the lower legs and feet after 5 minutes of standing; resolves completely upon lying down. Neurological: Grossly intact. DIAGNOSTIC TETRAD: Postural lightheadedness/presyncope, Extreme tachycardia on standing, Dependent acrocyanosis, Heart rate increase >30 bpm on tilt-table (confirmed on clinical active stand testing).
Environmental Observations: Multiple water bottles by the bed; patient is clearly attempting to self-manage.
Medication Reconciliation: No routine medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Postural Orthostatic Tachycardia Syndrome (POTS) - Status: Exacerbated (Severely Symptomatic)
Differentials:

Orthostatic Hypotension - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Active stand testing demonstrated a massive heart rate spike, but the blood pressure remained completely stable (actually rising slightly), ruling out true hypotension.

Inappropriate Sinus Tachycardia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The tachycardia is entirely and strictly postural, completely resolving when the patient returns to a supine position.

Generalized Anxiety Disorder / Panic Attack - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The objective, massive, reproducible heart rate spike tied purely to gravity/posture, combined with physical dependent acrocyanosis.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient exhibits the hallmark features of POTS: a massive, sustained postural tachycardia (>30 bpm increase) without orthostatic hypotension, accompanied by severe presyncope and dependent acrocyanosis. The strict postural nature and immediate relief upon lying down confirm a profound autonomic dysfunction rather than a primary cardiac arrhythmia. Urgent clinical evaluation was necessary to perform orthostatic testing safely in the home, confidently rule out dangerous arrhythmias or severe dehydration, and implement immediate, aggressive fluid/salt loading and counter-maneuvers to allow the patient safe mobilization to the bathroom and kitchen.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Educated the patient on the benign, though debilitating, nature of the dysautonomia. Recommended aggressive fluid loading (3 liters of water daily) and high sodium intake (up to 10g daily) to expand intravascular volume.
Positive Behavioral Change: Instructed the patient to put on high-grade, waist-high compression tights before getting out of bed every morning. By physically compressing the lower body, the patient prevents venous pooling upon standing. The reward is a dramatically lower heart rate spike and the ability to stand long enough to perform basic daily tasks without feeling faint.
Home-Based Services: None.
DME/Safety: Use a shower chair immediately to avoid prolonged standing in hot environments (which worsens vasodilation).
Follow-up: Routine General Practice review in 1-2 weeks for formal cardiology/autonomic specialist referral and potential pharmacological therapy (e.g., beta-blockers, fludrocortisone).

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the patient actually loses consciousness (true syncope), or if the heart remains racing rapidly (>120 bpm) for more than 30 minutes after lying completely flat, they must seek emergency medical care.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis relies on clinical active stand testing; formal tilt-table testing may be required by specialists; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: POTS is a chronic but manageable condition of the autonomic nervous system. Treatment relies heavily on lifestyle modifications, physical conditioning, and sometimes medication.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Always wear compression garments when out of bed. Increase salt and water intake heavily. Seek emergency care if your heart races uncontrollably while lying flat.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your autonomic nervous system is struggling to adjust to gravity. When you stand, blood pools in your legs (causing the purple color). To compensate and keep blood flowing to your brain, your heart beats frantically fast, causing the dizziness and tremors.
Actionable Steps: Drink an extra liter of salty water today. Do not stand up in the shower; use a chair. Buy waist-high compression tights tomorrow.

